# SBARR.OBJ - the four 23 mm barrels, their OWN model because they carry a
# second joint. MODELDRAW pitches this group about its TRUNNION and then
# yaws the result with the turret's bearing, so the guns ride the drum
# round and tilt at the mount - which is what a real gun mount does, and
# what one transform per model could never express.
#
# The trunnion is at engine (y 29, z 15) - the barrel roots. Everything
# here is measured from the same origin as the turret so the group bolts
# straight on.
#
# Same frame and scale as the rest of the vehicle: -Z forward, Y up, one
# unit = 0.2 m, at -s 3.
#
# 12 vertices / 6 faces.
o sbarr

# ---- the four barrels. Each is one tapered TRIANGLE lying in the
# horizontal plane - the plane a pilot actually looks down on - rooted
# inside the drum at z=-5 and drawn to a muzzle point at z=-16. Two of
# them at y=8.3, two at y=10.9: the stack of pairs from the drawing.
# The pairs are set 2.6 apart (0.52 m, wider than the real gun) so the
# cradle cheeks below have something to BE: at 0.32 m the whole assembly
# vanished into one grey line when the vehicle was seen level-on.
v 0.8000 8.3000 -5.0000
v 2.4000 8.3000 -5.0000
v 1.6000 8.3000 -16.0000
v 3.8000 8.3000 -5.0000
v 5.4000 8.3000 -5.0000
v 4.6000 8.3000 -16.0000
v 0.8000 10.9000 -5.0000
v 2.4000 10.9000 -5.0000
v 1.6000 10.9000 -16.0000
v 3.8000 10.9000 -5.0000
v 5.4000 10.9000 -5.0000
v 4.6000 10.9000 -16.0000

# ---- the guns. Four dark barrels for the view from ABOVE, which is the
# one a pilot gets; two LIGHT cradle cheeks for the view from the side,
# which is the one a gun run ends in. The cheeks cost NO new vertices -
# they are stitched from the barrels' own corners - and they are the whole
# reason the assembly does not vanish when the vehicle is seen level-on.
usemtl PAL3
f 1 2 3
f 4 5 6
f 7 8 9
f 10 11 12
usemtl PAL0
f 1 3 9 7
f 5 6 12 11
